<span class="kicker">2026-08-27 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Keep Mulch From Washing Onto a Paver Walkway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>Mulch migrating onto a paver walkway is one of those small, recurring annoyances that adds up over time, tracked into the house, swept off the same spot every week, or slowly building up in the joints until it starts holding moisture against the pavers. </p>
<p>The good news is that this is almost always a fixable problem, and the fix is rarely complicated. In the vast majority of cases, mulch washes onto a walkway because nothing is physically containing it, not because of some unusual drainage issue that needs a major intervention.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we deal with this exact situation regularly across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, usually on properties where a planting bed sits directly against a walkway with no barrier between them. This guide covers the containment solutions that actually solve the problem, how different mulch types behave differently, and how to fix a walkway that’s already dealing with this issue.</p>
<h2><b>Why Mulch Ends Up on Your Walkway</b></h2>
<p>Mulch beds sitting directly against a paver walkway, with no edging or barrier separating the two, rely entirely on the mulch staying put on its own, which it generally doesn’t do for long. Rain, wind, foot traffic brushing against the bed’s edge, and simple gravity on any bed with even a slight grade toward the walkway all contribute to gradual migration. Without something physically stopping that movement, it’s really just a matter of time before mulch starts showing up where you don’t want it.</p>
<h2><b>The Core Fix: Physical Edge Containment</b></h2>
<h3><b>Why Barriers Work Better Than Hoping for the Best</b></h3>
<p>The most reliable fix for mulch migration isn’t a different watering schedule or a steeper mulch pile, it’s a physical barrier between the bed and the walkway that mulch simply can’t cross without being obviously out of place. Once that barrier is in place, occasional mulch that does escape tends to sit visibly against the barrier rather than spreading onto the paver surface itself, making cleanup far easier when it does happen.</p>
<h2><b>Edging and Barrier Options Compared</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Option</b></td><td><b>Effectiveness</b></td><td><b>Look</b></td><td><b>Maintenance</b></td></tr><tr><td>Steel or aluminum edging</td><td>Very high</td><td>Clean, minimal, nearly invisible</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Plastic landscape edging</td><td>Moderate to high</td><td>Utilitarian</td><td>Low, but can shift over time</td></tr><tr><td>Paver or brick border course</td><td>High</td><td>Defined, matches hardscape</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Natural stone edging</td><td>High</td><td>Rustic, organic</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Raised bed border (timber or block)</td><td>Very high</td><td>Structured, defined bed</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>No barrier, mulch graded downward</td><td>Low</td><td>Seamless, blended look</td><td>High</td></tr></tbody></table><h3><b>Steel or Aluminum Edging</b></h3>
<p>Thin metal edging installed flush or slightly proud of the soil line creates a nearly invisible but highly effective barrier. It holds a clean line well and, because it’s so minimal visually, doesn’t compete with the mulch bed or the walkway for attention. This is often the best option when you want strong containment without adding a visually heavy element to the design.</p>
<h3><b>Plastic Landscape Edging</b></h3>
<p>A lower cost option that provides a similar function to metal edging, though it’s generally less rigid and more prone to shifting or heaving over time, particularly through freeze and thaw cycles common along the Front Range. It works reasonably well for smaller beds or lower-budget projects, but tends to need more frequent adjustment than metal.</p>
<h3><b>Paver or Brick Border Course</b></h3>
<p>Using a border course of pavers or brick, set slightly higher than the mulch bed, doubles as both a design element and a functional barrier. This ties directly into your walkway’s own material palette, which our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-finish-off-the-sides-of-a-paver-walkway/"> finishing off the sides of a paver walkway</a> covers in more detail as one of several border and transition approaches.</p>
<h3><b>Natural Stone Edging</b></h3>
<p>Larger stones or a low stacked stone border function similarly to a paver border course but read as more rustic and organic, pairing especially well with mountain-inspired landscaping common across the Denver area.</p>
<h3><b>Raised Bed Border</b></h3>
<p>A more substantial option, a low timber or block border that actually raises the planting bed slightly above the walkway grade, gives mulch a physical lip to stay behind rather than just a thin line to cross. This works particularly well for beds that see heavier rain runoff or wind exposure.</p>
<h2><b>Choosing Mulch That Resists Washing</b></h2>
<p>Beyond containment, the mulch material itself plays a real role in how prone it is to migrating in the first place.</p>
<ul><li><b>Shredded bark mulch</b> — the most common type, but also one of the more prone to washing, since individual pieces are lightweight and can float or get pushed by water relatively easily</li><li><b>Nugget or chunk bark mulch</b> — larger, heavier pieces resist washing better than shredded bark, though they can still shift with heavy rain on a sloped bed</li><li><b>River rock or decorative stone</b> — very resistant to washing due to weight, though it can still shift or scatter under significant water flow on a steep grade</li><li><b>Rubber mulch</b> — heavier than bark and resists washing well, though it’s a less common aesthetic choice for a traditional garden bed</li><li><b>Pine straw</b> — tends to mat together somewhat, which offers moderate resistance to washing, though individual strands can still migrate over time</li></ul>
<p>For a bed with any noticeable grade toward the walkway, choosing a heavier mulch type, nugget bark or decorative stone rather than fine shredded bark, meaningfully reduces how much material moves even before considering edging.</p>

